      "page_title": "Autovacuum: Tuning the Cleanup Loop",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Rows",
            "Dead needed",
            "Verdict"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "10k",
              "~2k",
              "fine"
            ],
            [
              "1M",
              "~200k",
              "late"
            ],
            [
              "100M",
              "~20M",
              "far too late"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Fix Big Tables Per Table"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "ALTER TABLE big SET (",
            "  autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor = 0.01,",
            "  autovacuum_vacuum_threshold = 10000);",
            "-- table overrides beat any global tuning"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Set aggressive values only on the few hot tables",
            "PG13+ adds `autovacuum_vacuum_insert_threshold`",
            "That gets append-only tables frozen and vm-updated",
            "ANALYZE has its own separate trigger pair"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Cost-Based Throttling"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Setting",
            "Default"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`vacuum_cost_limit`",
              "200 credits"
            ],
            [
              "`autovacuum_vacuum_cost_delay`",
              "2ms (PG12+)"
            ],
            [
              "`vacuum_cost_page_hit`",
              "1"
            ],
            [
              "`vacuum_cost_page_miss`",
              "2 (10 pre-PG14)"
            ],
            [
              "`vacuum_cost_page_dirty`",
              "20"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "On SSD",
          "text": "The default limit is far too conservative. Raising `vacuum_cost_limit` to 1000-2000 is routine."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Yielding, And Not"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Autovacuum cancels itself when DDL takes a conflicting lock",
            "Exception: wraparound-prevention mode never yields",
            "Never set `autovacuum = off`",
            "Outcome is unbounded bloat, then a forced wraparound shutdown"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "SELECT pid, query, backend_start FROM pg_stat_activity",
            "WHERE query LIKE 'autovacuum%';",
            "-- and in postgresql.conf:",
            "log_autovacuum_min_duration = 0  -- log every run"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Best Single Setting",
          "items": [
            "`log_autovacuum_min_duration = 0`",
            "Gives every run, its pages, its timing",
            "Most useful vacuum observability knob there is"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "A Circular Counter",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "32 bits = about 4.2 billion xids",
            "'Past' and 'future' are decided modulo-2^32",
            "~2 billion values behind you look past",
            "~2 billion ahead look future"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "The Failure",
          "text": "If the counter laps a tuple's xmin, that row looks like it is from the future and VANISHES. That is wraparound data loss."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Wraparound and Freezing",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Freezing Prevents It"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Mark a tuple's xmin frozen: unconditionally visible, forever",
            "Counter position stops mattering for that row",
            "Modern Postgres sets an infomask frozen bit",
            "It no longer writes xid 2, so xmin survives for forensics"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Three Ages"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Setting",
            "Default",
            "Effect"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`vacuum_freeze_min_age`",
              "50M",
              "freeze old tuples on scanned pages"
            ],
            [
              "`vacuum_freeze_table_age`",
              "150M",
              "aggressive scan of all-visible pages"
            ],
            [
              "`autovacuum_freeze_max_age`",
              "200M",
              "anti-wraparound run, even if autovacuum off"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Anti-wraparound vacuum does NOT yield to lock conflicts",
            "That is why a DDL-heavy table can suddenly block",
            "At ~40M xids left, Postgres logs warnings",
            "At ~3M it refuses writes: single-user mode VACUUM only"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Monitor Constantly"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "SELECT datname, age(datfrozenxid)",
            "FROM pg_database ORDER BY 2 DESC;",
            "SELECT relname, age(relfrozenxid) FROM pg_class",
            "WHERE relkind='r' ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 20;"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Second Counter"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "MultiXactIds have their own 32-bit counter, `pg_multixact`",
            "Own knob: `autovacuum_multixact_freeze_max_age`",
            "Own wraparound risk — monitor it too"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What Blocks Freezing"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "tree",
          "spec": {
            "root": "frozen horizon stuck",
            "branches": [
              {
                "label": "long txn",
                "children": [
                  "idle in transaction"
                ]
              },
              {
                "label": "replication",
                "children": [
                  "abandoned slot",
                  "hot_standby_feedback"
                ]
              },
              {
                "label": "2PC",
                "children": [
                  "unresolved prepared txn"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Wraparound and Freezing (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Emergency Gears",
          "items": [
            "PG14 failsafe: past `vacuum_failsafe_age` drop cost delays",
            "It also skips index cleanup to finish freezing fast",
            "PG17 TID store and PG18 async I/O cut the scan's wall clock"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Horizon Holders",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "1. Idle-in-transaction connection blocks ALL cleanup database-wide",
            "Fix: `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout`",
            "2. Unused replication slot pins xmin forever — drop dead slots",
            "3. `hot_standby_feedback = on` lets a slow replica hold the primary back",
            "4. A forgotten `pg_prepared_xacts` entry freezes the horizon"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "SELECT pid, state, xact_start,",
            "       now() - xact_start AS age",
            "FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE state <> 'idle'",
            "ORDER BY xact_start;  -- oldest culprit first"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Operational Mistakes"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "5. A churning queue table with no per-table tuning bloats 100x",
            "6. VACUUM FULL on production in business hours — use `pg_repack`",
            "7. Batch DELETE of millions, no follow-up VACUUM: file stays at peak",
            "8. `SELECT count(*)` is slow — MVCC checks visibility per row"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "No Free Row Count",
          "text": "No engine-level count exists, because no single row count is true for all snapshots at once."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Trade-off Table"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Engine",
            "Wins",
            "Pays"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Postgres in-heap",
              "free rollback, no undo contention",
              "bloat, mandatory vacuum"
            ],
            [
              "InnoDB undo log",
              "compact tables",
              "rollback scales with work"
            ],
            [
              "Oracle rollback seg",
              "compact tables",
              "'snapshot too old'"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Postgres readers never fail with 'snapshot too old'",
            "The undo log itself can grow unbounded too",
            "Every design pays somewhere — pick which somewhere"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Vacuum Is A Feature"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A fresh visibility map is what enables index-only scans",
            "Vacuum is a performance feature, not just cleanup",
            "Tune it like you tune indexes"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "cont": true
    },
